// Concept texts, batch 4 (names SPIRITUAL BYPASSING to EXHIBITIONISM).
// Row: [name, group code, short, feels, looks, misreading, help]. An empty help text uses the group's default.
// Group codes: P phobia, A anxiety, M mood, D disorder, T thinking pattern, C control or manipulation tactic,
// R trait or relationship pattern, V harmful habit, U strength or virtue, K practice or tool,
// F fascination or urge, B body or medical.
export default [
  ["SPIRITUAL BYPASSING", "T", "Using spiritual ideas to avoid painful feelings, conflicts or responsibilities.", "Spiritual phrases can bring quick comfort. Hard feelings may stay under the surface, and guilt or confusion can grow over time.", "Saying 'everything happens for a reason' when someone is hurting, or changing the subject to a spiritual idea.", "Faith and spiritual practice can support healing. The pattern is harmful when they replace honest feelings or needed action.", ""],
  ["DERMATILLOMANIA", "D", "An older name for a compulsive urge to pick at the skin, leaving sores or scars.", "Picking can bring a brief sense of relief or focus. Shame and frustration often follow, and the urge may return quickly.", "Scabs, marks or bandages on the skin, and repeated picking at the same few spots.", "It is a real condition, not a sign of weakness or vanity. A doctor or therapist can help, and habit-reversal therapy has helped many people.", "If picking feels like self-harm, or you have thoughts of ending your life, call or text 988 in the US now, and call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["DESPAIRPHILE", "F", "A pull toward hopeless or despairing stories, music, images or ideas.", "Dark, hopeless themes can feel strangely gripping. Some people feel less alone when their pain is reflected in art.", "Seeking out sad or hopeless content often, or talking about hopelessness as if nothing else is true.", "Sad art can help people feel understood. It becomes a concern when it deepens hopelessness or brings thoughts of ending your life.", "If you have thoughts of ending your life, call or text 988 in the US now, and call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["DETERMINED", "U", "Firmly committed to a goal, even when the path is hard and slow.", "Determination can feel steady and focused. It gives energy when progress is slow and rewards feel far away.", "Returning to a task after setbacks, and keeping steady effort day after day.", "Determination does not mean ignoring rest or never changing course. Good goals can be adjusted as you learn more.", ""],
  ["DEVALUATION", "C", "Repeatedly belittling someone to lower their confidence and gain control over them.", "The target may feel small, confused and anxious to please. Self-worth can wear down slowly, often without a clear moment of harm.", "Put-downs, mocking remarks, and praise that is quickly followed by criticism.", "Honest disagreement is not devaluation. The pattern is harmful when put-downs are repeated to make someone feel worthless.", ""],
  ["DEVALUER", "R", "A person who repeatedly puts others down to feel superior or in control.", "Underneath, the devaluer may feel threatened and need to win. Others often feel they can never get things right.", "Constant criticism, dismissive comments, and sudden coldness after praise.", "Everyone can say something unkind on a bad day. The pattern is harmful when belittling becomes a regular way to feel in control.", ""],
  ["DEVELOPMENTAL TRAUMA", "D", "Lasting harm from repeated stress, abuse or neglect during childhood.", "Early harm can shape how safe the world feels. It may bring anxiety, shame, or trouble trusting others, even when things are calm now.", "Strong reactions to stress, trouble in close relationships, and difficulty calming down after upset.", "It is not a personal failing or a fixed fate. Trauma-focused care and steady, safe relationships can help people feel more secure.", ""],
  ["DEVOTED", "U", "Loyal and committed to people, causes or beliefs you care about deeply.", "Devotion can feel warm, steady and meaningful. It can also bring worry when someone you care for is struggling.", "Showing up consistently, keeping promises, and staying close during hard times.", "Devotion is healthy when it is freely chosen. It does not require giving up your own needs or staying in a harmful situation.", ""],
  ["DIAPHRAGMATIC BREATHING", "K", "A slow breathing practice that uses the belly and the large muscle under the lungs.", "Slow breathing can ease tension and calm a racing heart. It may feel awkward at first, then more natural with practice.", "Calm, low breathing where the belly rises and falls, with relaxed shoulders and jaw.", "Breathing slowly does not fix every problem on its own. If you feel dizzy or lightheaded, go back to normal breathing and rest.", ""],
  ["DIARRHEA OR CONSTIPATION (STRESS-INDUCED)", "B", "Looser or harder bowel movements that flare up when stress is high.", "Stress can upset the gut, with cramps, urgency or heaviness. Worry about finding a bathroom can add to anxiety.", "Frequent bathroom trips, leaving events early, or canceling plans during stressful times.", "Stress can trigger real gut symptoms. Ongoing changes, blood in the stool, or fever need a doctor, since other causes need care.", ""],
  ["DIGESTIVE ISSUES", "B", "Ongoing stomach or gut problems, such as pain, bloating, nausea or irregular bowels.", "Digestive trouble can be uncomfortable, unpredictable and tiring. It can shape plans, sleep and mood.", "Leaving events early, planning around bathroom access, or seeming tired and quiet on bad days.", "Gut symptoms are real, not imagined. Stress can make them worse, and a doctor can check for causes and treatments.", ""],
  ["DILIGENCE", "U", "Careful, steady effort in work, study or caring for others over time.", "Diligence can feel satisfying, with pride in steady progress. It can also bring fatigue when rest is pushed aside.", "Checking details, finishing tasks on time, and following through on commitments.", "Diligence is not perfectionism or nonstop effort. It works best with rest, realistic goals and room to make mistakes.", ""],
  ["DILIGENT", "U", "A person who works carefully and reliably, and keeps going with what matters.", "Being diligent can feel grounded and dependable. It can also bring pressure to keep up with high standards at all times.", "Arriving prepared, keeping track of details, and doing what was promised.", "Being diligent is a strength, not a reason to carry every task alone. Asking for help is also part of being responsible.", ""],
  ["DIOGENES SYNDROME", "D", "Severe self-neglect and hoarding, often seen in older adults who pull away from others.", "The person may feel overwhelmed, ashamed or unable to see the mess as a problem. Withdrawing from others can grow over time.", "Piles of items, neglected hygiene, unopened mail, and pulling away from neighbors and family.", "It is not simply laziness or stubbornness. It can have medical, memory or mental health causes, and supportive care can help.", "If you or someone else cannot stay safe, call 911 if you are in danger. You can also call or text 988 in the US now for support."],
  ["DIPSOMANIAC", "V", "An old term for a strong, repeated urge to drink alcohol, often in binges.", "Drinking can feel urgent and hard to resist. Regret, shame and anxiety often follow, and the urge may return soon after.", "Heavy drinking in bouts, hidden bottles, and missed work, plans or family time.", "Stopping heavy drinking suddenly can be dangerous for some people. Talk with a doctor first, because treatment can help many people cut back or stop safely.", ""],
  ["DISCARD", "R", "Dropping someone suddenly and coldly, with little explanation or care for how they feel.", "Being dropped without warning can feel like a sudden loss of ground. Doubt about your own worth can follow for a long time.", "Ending contact abruptly, ignoring messages, and moving on quickly as if nothing happened.", "Being discarded does not prove that you are unworthy. It reflects how the other person chose to treat you.", ""],
  ["DISCIPLINED", "U", "Keeping steady habits and following through, even when motivation runs low.", "Discipline can feel steady and satisfying, with quiet pride in keeping promises. It can also feel tiring on hard days.", "Regular routines, finishing tasks on time, and sticking to plans with little drama.", "Discipline is not harsh punishment or nonstop self-control. It works best when it is gentle, realistic and balanced with rest.", ""],
  ["DISINHIBITED SOCIAL ENGAGEMENT DISORDER", "D", "A condition in some young children who approach strangers too readily, without usual caution.", "The child may not feel the usual wariness around unfamiliar adults. This can leave them exposed to harm, even when the adult seems friendly.", "Going off with unfamiliar adults, seeking comfort from almost anyone, and little hesitation with strangers.", "It is not a child's fault, and it is not simply bad parenting. Close supervision, a steady caregiver and specialist support can make a real difference.", ""],
  ["DISMISSIVE-AVOIDANT", "R", "A way of relating that keeps others at arm's length and downplays the need for closeness.", "Closeness can feel uncomfortable, so pulling back may feel safer when feelings grow. Self-reliance can feel like the only option.", "Keeping emotional distance, downplaying your own needs, and avoiding deep conversations.", "It does not mean the person does not care. It is often a learned way of staying safe, and it can soften with trust.", ""],
  ["DISORDER", "D", "A general term for a condition that disrupts how the mind, body or behavior works.", "The word can feel heavy and label-like. It may bring worry about being broken or different from others.", "Symptoms that get in the way of work, school, sleep or relationships over time.", "A disorder is a set of symptoms that can be treated or managed. It is not a judgment of your worth.", ""],
  ["DISORGANIZED", "T", "A pattern of scattered focus, loose routines and tasks left unfinished.", "Life can feel scattered, and starting or finishing things may be hard. Each loose end can add to stress.", "Missed deadlines, cluttered spaces, lost items, and trouble keeping track of plans.", "Being disorganized is not laziness or a lack of intelligence. Simple systems, reminders and support often help a lot.", ""],
  ["DISORGANIZED ATTACHMENT", "R", "A pattern of wanting closeness while also fearing it, often after unpredictable care.", "Closeness can feel both needed and dangerous. Feelings may swing from clinging to pulling away, often without warning.", "Hot and cold responses in relationships, and confusion about how to get close safely.", "It is not a choice to be chaotic. It grows from confusing early experiences, and steady relationships and therapy can help over time.", ""],
  ["DISOWNED SHADOW", "T", "The parts of yourself you reject or hide, such as anger, envy or neediness.", "Hidden parts can show up as sudden irritation or harsh judgment of others. The hidden part may feel shameful or unsafe to show.", "Strong criticism of others for traits you may also carry, or sharp reactions to particular people.", "Having a shadow is part of being human. Getting to know these parts can bring more honesty and less inner conflict.", ""],
  ["DISPLACED ANGER", "M", "Anger aimed at a safer or unrelated person instead of the real source.", "The anger may feel sudden and too big for the moment. The real source can feel too risky or painful to face.", "Snapping at family or coworkers after a stressful call with someone else in charge.", "Anger is a normal feeling. Displaced anger can still hurt people who did nothing wrong, so naming the real source helps.", ""],
  ["DISPLACEMENT", "T", "Shifting a feeling or impulse from its real target to an easier, safer one.", "The original feeling can remain even when it is aimed elsewhere. Relief may come quickly, then confusion about why the anger is so strong.", "Taking stress out on someone who was not the cause, or pouring energy into a safer task.", "Displacement is a common mind habit, not a secret plan. Naming the real source makes it easier to respond clearly.", ""],
  ["DISRUPTIVE MOOD DYSREGULATION DISORDER", "D", "A childhood condition with frequent, severe temper outbursts and ongoing irritability.", "The child may feel overwhelmed and misunderstood. Anger can rise fast, and calm can take a long time to return.", "Frequent, intense temper outbursts, and a constant irritable mood between them.", "It is not caused by willfulness alone. Specialized therapy and parent support can make a real difference for the child and family.", ""],
  ["DISSOCIATED", "D", "Feeling detached from your thoughts, body or surroundings, as if you are not fully there.", "Familiar things can seem distant or muffled. The body may feel far away, which can be unsettling and frightening.", "Staring into space, slow replies, or seeming not fully present in conversation.", "Brief detachment is common under stress or tiredness. Repeated episodes deserve a doctor's or therapist's review.", ""],
  ["DISSOCIATION", "D", "A split between thoughts, feelings, memory, body or sense of self, often under stress.", "It can feel like watching life from a distance, or like missing pieces of time. Many people find it confusing and lonely.", "Blank stares, sudden changes of topic, or gaps in remembering recent events.", "Dissociation is often a protective response to overwhelming stress or past harm. It is not a sign of weakness, and help can make it less frequent.", ""],
  ["DISSOCIATION GROUNDING", "K", "A practice that uses your senses and body to return to the present moment.", "Grounding can bring a sense of solid ground and of being here. The feeling of unreality may ease bit by bit.", "Pressing feet into the floor, naming objects in the room, or holding something cool.", "Grounding does not erase the experience. It helps you feel safer while it passes, and a therapist can teach more methods.", ""],
  ["DISSOCIATIVE AMNESIA", "D", "Gaps in memory for important personal events, often after stress or trauma.", "Missing memories can feel frightening and confusing. You may sense something is missing without knowing what it is.", "Trouble recalling key events or personal details, especially when reminded of stressful times.", "It is not weakness, and it is not simply lying. A doctor can check for medical causes, and a trauma-informed therapist can help.", ""],
  ["DISSOCIATIVE FUGUE", "D", "A sudden trip away from home with memory loss about who you are.", "On waking, the person may feel lost and confused about where they are and how they got there. Sudden gaps can be frightening.", "Leaving home or work suddenly, traveling far, and not remembering who they are for a time.", "It is not a choice or a plan to disappear. It is a rare response to severe stress and needs prompt medical and psychiatric care.", "If you or someone else is missing or unsafe, you can call or text 988 in the US now, and call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["DISSOCIATIVE IDENTITY DISORDER", "D", "A condition in which a person's sense of self is split into separate identity states.", "Shifting between parts of self can feel confusing, and time may go missing. The person may feel unsure who is in control.", "Gaps in memory, sudden shifts in mood or behavior, and talk of different inner selves.", "It is not the dramatic version shown in movies, and it is not the same as being dangerous to others. Trauma-focused care can help people feel more connected within themselves.", ""],
  ["DISSOCIATIVE PERSONALITY DISORDER", "D", "An older, informal term for a split sense of self, linked with dissociative identity disorder.", "Parts of the self can feel separate, and it may be hard to know who you are in a given moment. Time can slip away.", "Gaps in memory, shifts in mood or behavior, and feeling out of step with one's own life.", "It is not a lack of willpower or a character flaw. Specialized care can help a person build a steadier sense of self over time.", ""],
  ["DIVIDE AND CONQUER", "C", "Splitting people apart and turning them against each other, so none can stand together.", "Being turned against others can feel isolating and confusing. Trust in friends or family can slowly break down.", "Telling each person a different story, spreading rumors, or encouraging rivalry between friends.", "Disagreements between people are normal. The tactic is harmful when it is used to keep people apart and easier to control.", ""],
  ["DIZZINESS", "B", "A spinning, swaying or light-headed feeling that can come from many causes.", "The room may seem to move, or your head may feel floaty and unsteady. It can be scary, especially when it comes on suddenly.", "Holding a wall or a chair, sitting down suddenly, or pausing before walking.", "Dizziness is a symptom, not a diagnosis. It can come from dehydration, low blood pressure, medicines or inner ear problems, and frequent episodes deserve a doctor's check.", ""],
  ["DOMINEERING", "R", "Controlling others by overpowering them with commands, pressure or harsh reactions.", "Others may feel pushed aside, afraid to disagree or unable to speak freely. The domineering person may feel anxious if they lose control.", "Interrupting, giving orders, ending discussions early, and punishing disagreement.", "Confident leadership is not always domineering. The pattern is harmful when it silences others and ignores their choices.", ""],
  ["DOMINOPHILE", "F", "A deep love of dominoes, the tiles and the games played with them.", "Dominoes can feel satisfying, with the click of tiles and the pattern of a game. Some people feel calm while playing.", "Collecting sets, learning new patterns, and spending long hours on domino games.", "Enjoying a game is healthy. It becomes a concern only when it crowds out sleep, work or people you care about.", ""],
  ["DOOM SPIRALING", "T", "A runaway chain of worried thoughts that assumes the worst, one step after another.", "Thoughts pile up fast, each one darker than the last. The body may tense up, and sleep or focus can slip.", "Constant talk of worst cases, endless scrolling for bad news, and visible worry.", "A doom spiral feels like a forecast, but it is a thinking pattern. Pausing, checking facts and talking with someone can slow it down.", ""],
  ["DOOMED", "T", "A belief that a bad fate is certain and that nothing you do can change it.", "The belief can feel heavy and fixed, which can drain motivation. Trying may seem pointless, so you may stop before you start.", "Saying things are hopeless, expecting every good thing to be taken away, or giving up on plans early.", "A feeling of doom is a thought, not a fact about your future. Hopelessness often eases with treatment, so tell a doctor or therapist if it lingers.", "If you have thoughts of ending your life, call or text 988 in the US now, and call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["DOOMSCROLLING", "V", "A habit of scrolling through distressing news and posts, even when it makes you feel worse.", "Each new headline can bring a fresh jolt of fear. Stopping can feel hard, and anxiety often grows the longer it goes on.", "Staying on the phone late, checking news again and again, and talking mostly about bad events.", "Staying informed is useful. The habit becomes harmful when it drives fear and sleep loss, and it can be limited with set times.", ""],
  ["DOPPELGANGER", "D", "An experience of seeing or sensing a copy of yourself, a double that seems outside you.", "The double may feel eerie, unsettling or hard to explain. For a moment, the sense of self can seem split or strange.", "Someone may say they saw a double, felt a second presence, or looked at themselves from outside.", "It is not proof of being possessed or doomed. It can happen with stress, migraines, seizures, illness or mental health conditions, so a doctor should check it.", "You can call or text 988 in the US now for support, and you can call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger."],
  ["DOROMANIAC", "F", "A strong, repeated urge to give gifts, sometimes beyond what you can afford.", "The urge to give can feel warm and urgent. Relief often comes right after giving, then worry about money may follow.", "Frequent gifts, spending beyond your means, and distress when a gift is not accepted.", "Generosity is a virtue. The pattern becomes a problem when gifts create debt, stress or pressure on the people receiving them.", ""],
  ["DOUBLE", "R", "Keeping two versions of yourself or your life, one shown to others and one hidden.", "Keeping two selves can feel exhausting and lonely. Fear of being found out can bring constant tension.", "Telling different stories to different people, careful secrecy, and details that do not quite add up.", "Everyone keeps some things private, which is healthy. The pattern matters when hiding becomes the main way someone lives.", ""],
  ["DOUBLE BIND", "C", "A trap where every choice leads to blame, so there is no answer that feels right.", "You may feel stuck and guilty, whatever you do. Anxiety can grow because it feels like losing either way.", "Being criticized for both doing and not doing something, with mixed or contradictory messages.", "Sometimes people send mixed messages without meaning to. It is harmful when the trap is used to keep someone off balance and unable to choose.", ""],
  ["DOWN SYNDROME", "B", "A lifelong genetic condition that affects learning, growth and health.", "Learning may feel slower or harder at times. Many people with the condition feel joy, friendship and pride, just like anyone else.", "Learning new skills may take more time, and some people use speech, physical or occupational therapy.", "It does not define a whole life. Early support, schooling and health care help many people build full, meaningful lives.", ""],
  ["DROMOMANIAC", "F", "A strong urge to walk, wander or travel without a clear purpose.", "The urge can feel strong and hard to resist, and staying still may feel uncomfortable. Relief often comes only while moving.", "Restless walking, frequent trips, and trouble staying in one place for long.", "Liking to travel is common and healthy. The pattern becomes a concern when it causes harm, debt or missed responsibilities.", ""],
  ["DRY MOUTH (XEROSTOMIA)", "B", "A dry mouth, called xerostomia, caused by too little saliva, which keeps the mouth moist.", "A dry mouth can make talking, eating and swallowing harder. It can feel sticky and irritating through the day.", "Frequent sips of water, pausing to speak, or trouble swallowing dry foods.", "It can come from medicines, stress, breathing through the mouth or illness. A dentist or doctor can help find the cause and protect your teeth.", ""],
  ["DUCHENNE", "B", "Duchenne muscular dystrophy, a genetic condition that slowly weakens muscles, mostly in boys.", "Weakness and tiredness can limit movement. The person may feel frustrated or worried about the future.", "Trouble climbing stairs, frequent falls, and needing more help with daily movement as it progresses.", "It is not caused by a lack of effort or by poor care. Specialist teams, physical therapy and treatments can help with comfort, movement and planning.", ""],
  ["THE DYSMORPHIA", "D", "A distressing sense that part of your body is flawed, though others see nothing wrong.", "The flaw can feel huge and obvious. Reassurance may bring relief for a moment, then the worry comes back.", "Frequent mirror checks, camera avoidance, and long time spent on grooming or hiding.", "Others often cannot see the flaw that feels so large to you. This is a treatable condition, and therapy can loosen its grip.", ""],
  ["DYSMORPHOMANIAC", "T", "An older term for a strong preoccupation with flaws in one's appearance that others do not see.", "The preoccupation can take up hours in a day. Each glance in a mirror may bring a fresh wave of doubt.", "Repeated checking of reflections, asking others for reassurance about looks, and avoiding photos.", "It is not simply vanity, and the flaw in the thought may not match how others see you. A therapist can help you test the thought more calmly.", ""],
  ["DYSREGULATED", "M", "Feelings or body reactions that are hard to manage, rising fast and staying high.", "Emotions can surge quickly, and calming down can take a long time. The body may feel on edge or flooded.", "Sudden anger or tears, trouble settling down, and reactions that seem bigger than the moment.", "Being dysregulated is not a character flaw. Breathing, rest and support can ease the state, and skills can be built with practice.", ""],
  ["ECHO", "R", "Repeating back someone's words or actions, often to connect, agree or fill a silence.", "Echoing can feel like a way to connect or to avoid speaking your own mind. It may bring a sense of ease in a tense moment.", "Repeating the last few words someone said, or copying their phrases during conversation.", "Repeating words is often a normal way to show interest or think aloud. It needs attention when it is constant or linked to confusion.", ""],
  ["ECHOLALIC", "D", "A person who repeats words or phrases they have just heard, a pattern called echolalia.", "The words may slip out without a clear choice. For some people, repeating phrases feels calming or helps them communicate.", "Repeating phrases from TV, teachers or other people, often in the same tone they were heard.", "Echolalia is often a communication or self-soothing style, not a sign of low intelligence. A speech therapist or doctor can help find the best support.", ""],
  ["ECHOPRAXIC", "B", "A person who copies other people's movements without meaning to, a pattern called echopraxia.", "The copied movement can happen before the person decides to move. It may feel strange, or the person may not notice it at all.", "Mirroring gestures, steps or posture without any clear reason, often noticed by others first.", "It can appear with some brain, nerve or medical conditions, so a doctor should check any new or frequent copying.", ""],
  ["ECSTATIC", "M", "Intense, overflowing joy that feels bright, wild and almost beyond control.", "Joy can feel electric, with energy, laughter and a sense that anything is possible. It usually settles after a while.", "Laughing loudly, moving quickly, talking fast, or sharing excitement with everyone nearby.", "Brief ecstasy is a healthy part of life. If joy comes with little sleep, racing thoughts or risky spending, talk with a doctor.", ""],
  ["EFFORTLESS INNER PEACE", "U", "A calm that arrives without forcing and stays with you through ordinary days.", "Peace can feel light and unhurried, as if nothing needs fixing right now. Thoughts still come, but they pass without pulling you far.", "Relaxed breathing, a softer face, and a steady pace in conversation.", "Effortless peace is not a goal you must force or prove. It often comes from letting go of pressure, and it returns with practice.", ""],
  ["EFFORTLESS SELF-ACCEPTANCE", "U", "Accepting yourself as you are, without having to earn it or push yourself into it.", "Self-acceptance can feel light and steady. Harsh inner criticism may quiet for a while, giving room to breathe.", "Speaking to yourself with fairness, letting mistakes be human, and asking for help without shame.", "It is not giving up on growth. Accepting yourself as you are often makes change easier, not less likely.", ""],
  ["EISOPTROPHOBE", "P", "A person with a strong fear of mirrors, or of seeing their own reflection.", "Seeing a reflection can bring a sudden jolt of fear. The heart may race, and there may be a strong urge to look away.", "Avoiding mirrors, covering reflective surfaces, or leaving rooms that have them.", "It is a real fear and can be treated. Gradual exposure with a therapist often helps people feel calmer around reflections.", ""],
  ["EKBOM SYNDROME", "D", "A firm belief that insects or parasites live under the skin, even when tests find none.", "The skin may feel itchy, crawling or stinging, and the belief can feel completely real. Clear test results can be frustrating and frightening.", "Scratching or picking at the skin, bringing samples to others, and asking for pest treatments.", "The sensations feel real to the person, but they often come from nerve, skin or medical causes rather than insects. Calm, careful care helps.", "You can call or text 988 in the US now for support. You can also call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger."],
  ["ELATED", "M", "Feeling high, joyful and carried away, often with extra energy and quick thoughts.", "Elation can feel bright, light and full of energy. Thoughts may come faster than usual, and plans can feel endless.", "Talking quickly, starting many projects at once, and needing much less sleep than usual.", "A happy high can be healthy. When it lasts for days, or comes with risky choices, it may be a sign of a mood episode that needs a doctor.", ""],
  ["ELEVATED BLOOD PRESSURE", "B", "Blood pressure that stays above the healthy range, often without any clear symptoms.", "Many people feel nothing at all. Some notice a pounding heartbeat or a flushed face, though these can have other causes.", "Usually hidden from others, and often found only at a routine checkup or a home reading.", "A high reading is not a sign of weakness, and one stressful moment does not define it. Regular checks, movement and a doctor's plan can lower the risk of heart attack and stroke.", ""],
  ["EMBODIED CONFIDENCE", "U", "Feeling self-assured through the body, with steady breath, grounded feet and a sense of readiness.", "Confidence can show up as a steady chest and a sense of being ready. It can calm the nerves before you speak or act.", "Standing with an even stance, speaking at a steady pace, and holding eye contact comfortably.", "It is not pretending or forcing a big posture. It is noticing the body's sense of ground and building from there.", ""],
  ["EMBODIED GRATITUDE", "U", "Feeling thankful through the body, such as warmth in the chest and a slower breath.", "Gratitude can bring a warm feeling in the chest and a softer, slower breath. It can ease a tight or guarded mood.", "A gentler voice, saying thank you with eye contact, or pausing to notice good things.", "Gratitude does not mean ignoring pain or pretending everything is fine. It can sit alongside grief, anger or worry.", ""],
  ["EMBODIED INTUITION", "U", "Sensing what is right through body signals, such as a settled feeling in the gut.", "Intuition can feel like a steady inner sense, sometimes felt in the stomach or chest. It often feels calmer than anxious thinking.", "Pausing before a decision, or choosing slowly after noticing how the body responds.", "Body signals are useful clues, not proof. Check them against facts, advice and past experience before acting.", ""],
  ["EMBODIED JOY", "U", "Joy felt through the body, in warmth, lightness, movement and easy breathing.", "Joy can show up as lightness in the chest, looser shoulders and a wish to move or smile. It can feel simple and clear.", "Smiling, dancing or stretching, laughing easily, and enjoying small sensations like sunlight or warm water.", "Joy does not need to be constant or intense. Small moments of pleasure in the body still count.", ""],
  ["EMBODIED MOVEMENT", "K", "A practice of moving the body with attention, such as slow walking, stretching or dancing.", "Gentle movement can release tension and lift mood. It may feel awkward at first, then more natural with time.", "Slow walks with attention on each step, gentle stretching, or easy dancing at home.", "It does not have to be intense exercise to help. Check with a doctor first if you have an injury or heart concern.", ""],
  ["EMBODIED SELF-COMPASSION", "K", "A practice of treating your body and feelings with kindness, through touch or gentle words.", "A warm touch or kind words can soften shame and calm a racing body. The feeling may be small at first, then grow.", "A hand placed on the chest, a gentle tone when speaking to yourself, and pausing before judging.", "It is not indulging or avoiding responsibility. It is a steady way to meet hard feelings without adding harsh criticism.", ""],
  ["EMBODIED THRIVING", "U", "Feeling alive and well in the body, with steady energy and a sense of health.", "Thriving can feel steady and grounded, with enough energy for daily life. Good days and rest days both feel part of the picture.", "Regular sleep, daily movement, balanced meals, and taking part in activities that matter.", "Thriving does not mean being perfect or never tired. It is a steadier way of living that includes rest and recovery.", ""],
  ["EMETOPHILIA", "F", "A sexual interest linked to vomiting, which can bring shame or confusion.", "This interest can feel private and hard to talk about. It may bring shame, confusion or worry about what it means.", "Usually private, so others rarely notice it at all.", "Having an interest does not mean acting on it. Acting on it without consent harms other people, and help is available.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["EMETOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong fear of vomiting, or of seeing or being near someone who is vomiting.", "Nausea or the thought of being sick can bring a racing heart and sweating. The urge to escape can feel overwhelming.", "Avoiding certain places, staying away from sick people, or leaving events early.", "It is a real and treatable fear. Gradual exposure with a therapist often helps, and it does not mean you are weak.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL AVOIDANCE", "V", "A habit of steering away from painful feelings through busyness, distraction or silence.", "Avoiding a feeling brings quick relief, then it may return stronger. Life can feel narrower and more muted over time.", "Keeping busy, changing the subject, or avoiding people and places that stir feelings.", "Taking space from hard feelings can be wise for a while. It becomes harmful when avoidance is the only way you cope.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL BLACKMAIL", "C", "Using guilt, fear or threats to force another person to give in.", "The target may feel trapped, guilty and pressured to put the other person's needs first. Anxiety and self-doubt can grow.", "Threats to leave, sulking until the other person gives in, and reminders of past favors.", "Wanting something from someone is normal. The tactic is harmful when fear or guilt is used to take away a person's real choice.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL BURNOUT", "M", "A long drain of emotional energy from stress, caring or conflict, leaving you numb.", "Feelings can seem flat, and small tasks can feel heavy. Dread may come before ordinary days begin.", "Irritability, pulling away from friends, missed calls, and getting through the day on autopilot.", "It is not weakness or laziness. It is a sign that the load has been too heavy for too long, and rest and support are needed.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL CONTAINMENT", "K", "A practice of holding strong feelings in a safe container until you can work with them.", "Containment can bring a sense of order and safety. Feelings may feel less overwhelming when they have a clear place to go.", "Taking a pause, writing feelings down, or choosing a private time to talk them through.", "It is not suppression or denial. Containment means holding feelings safely for now, then giving them attention later.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL DISSOCIATION", "D", "A numb, detached feeling in which emotions seem far away, even when you want to feel them.", "Feelings can seem muffled or distant. Connection with others may feel hard to reach, which can be lonely.", "A flat tone, little reaction to big news, and seeming distant in close moments.", "It is often a protective response to stress or past harm, not a lack of care. Therapy can help feelings return gently at a safe pace.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL DYSREGULATION", "M", "Trouble managing how strong or how quick emotions are, so they swing easily.", "Emotions can rise fast and feel too big to manage. Calming down may take a long time, and the body can stay on edge.", "Sudden anger, crying or withdrawal after small triggers, and hard-to-predict moods.", "It is not a choice or a sign of weakness. Skills like breathing, naming feelings and support can build steadier responses over time.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL FLASHBACKS", "D", "Sudden waves of an old feeling, like fear or shame, that seem to be happening now.", "The old feeling can return with full force, even when the present is safe. It can bring panic, shame or a sense of being small.", "Sudden withdrawal, tearfulness or freezing after a small trigger, with little explanation.", "The reaction is real, but it comes from an old memory of feeling, not proof that danger is happening now. Trauma-focused therapy can help.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL FLOODING", "M", "Feelings rising so fast and strong that clear thinking becomes very hard.", "The body can race, and words may disappear in the moment. It can feel as if the mind has shut down.", "Shouting, freezing, or leaving the room suddenly during an argument.", "Flooding is a normal response to strong stress. Taking a break and returning when calmer can help both people.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL FLUIDITY", "U", "Moving between feelings with ease, without getting stuck in one for too long.", "Feelings can change and pass without fear. You can feel sad and still feel hope in the same day.", "Naming feelings openly, adjusting to changing moods, and recovering after an upset.", "Fluidity does not mean having no feelings or always feeling good. It means letting feelings move through you.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL LIBERATION", "U", "Freedom to feel and express emotions without fear of shame or punishment.", "Feelings can feel lighter and more honest. Tears or laughter may come more easily when the body feels safe.", "Speaking openly, showing feelings in safe places, and asking clearly for what is needed.", "Liberation does not mean reacting to everything without thought. It means feelings are allowed, then handled with care.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL MASTERY", "U", "Skill in understanding and guiding your emotions with calm and clear judgment.", "Emotions can feel more manageable. You can notice a strong feeling without being swept away by it.", "Pausing before reacting, naming feelings clearly, and choosing a response that fits the situation.", "Mastery does not mean never feeling strong emotions. It grows slowly, through practice and honest reflection.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL NEGLECT", "R", "Growing up without enough emotional care, attention or comfort when feelings were shown.", "Feelings may have seemed unimportant, so it can be hard to name needs. Loneliness can grow even in a busy home.", "Hesitating to ask for help, minimizing hurt, or staying quiet about what matters.", "Neglect does not mean you are unlovable. It shapes how people learn to care for themselves, and new relationships and therapy can help.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL NUMBING", "M", "A flat, muted state in which little emotion comes through, good or bad.", "Feelings can seem far away, and joy or sadness may be hard to reach. Life can look gray even on good days.", "Less reaction to good or bad news, quiet withdrawal, and going through routines on autopilot.", "Numbing is often a protection after stress or loss. It is not permanent, and support can help feelings return gently.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL NUMBING RELEASE", "K", "A practice of letting numbness thaw slowly, through small, safe moments of feeling.", "Feelings may return slowly, sometimes with tears or discomfort. This can feel scary at first, then relieving.", "Listening to music that moves you, writing a few honest lines, or sharing one feeling with a friend.", "It does not mean forcing feelings to return all at once. Pacing matters, and a therapist can help if it feels like too much.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL REGULATION MASTERY", "U", "Skill in calming and steering strong emotions in ways that help you and others.", "Emotions can feel more manageable, with more room between a feeling and a response. Recovery after upset becomes quicker.", "Taking a breath before speaking, asking for a short break, and returning calmly to the topic.", "It does not mean never being upset. It means recovering well and handling strong feelings with care.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL SUPPRESSION", "T", "Holding back or hiding feelings so they do not show or get acknowledged.", "Holding feelings in can feel safe at first, but tension may build. Closeness with others can start to feel distant.", "A flat expression, short replies, and steering away from talk about feelings.", "Choosing not to share a feeling at a certain time is healthy. Ongoing suppression can strain health and closeness.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL VAMPIRE", "R", "A label for someone whose constant crises or need for reassurance drain others.", "The other person may feel drained, guilty and unable to step away. Their own needs can get lost in the constant demands.", "Frequent crises, needing replies right away, and strong upset when others set limits.", "Everyone needs support at times. The pattern matters when one person's needs keep taking the other's energy with little balance.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONAL WITHHOLDING", "R", "Holding back warmth, affection or words, often to punish, control or pressure someone.", "The other person may feel unseen, anxious and unsure how to reconnect. Warmth seems to come and go without warning.", "Long silences, refusing affection, or withholding praise to influence what someone does.", "Needing space is healthy. Withholding warmth as punishment or control is harmful, and it often feels like rejection.", ""],
  ["EMOTIONALLY UNAVAILABLE", "R", "Unable or unwilling to share feelings or meet a partner's emotional needs.", "The partner may feel lonely, unsure or always reaching. Closeness can feel one-sided over time.", "Avoiding deep talks, pulling away when feelings rise, and offering little comfort.", "Not everyone is good at talking about feelings. The pattern matters when closeness stays out of reach, and it can change with honesty and support.", ""],
  ["EMPATH", "R", "A person who senses other people's feelings strongly and may take them on.", "Other people's moods can feel very strong, almost like your own. It can be draining and overwhelming on busy days.", "Quick to notice distress, offering comfort, and sometimes carrying others' problems for a long time.", "Sensing feelings is a real and helpful skill. Caring for your own limits matters as much as caring for others.", ""],
  ["EMPOWERED", "U", "Feeling capable and in charge of your own choices and your own direction.", "Empowerment can feel strong, clear and steady. It can bring energy to take the next step.", "Making decisions, speaking up, and acting on what matters to you.", "Feeling empowered does not mean never needing help. It means having a real voice and real choices in your own life.", ""],
  ["EMPOWERED INNER RESILIENCE", "U", "Inner strength that helps you recover from hard moments and keep your footing.", "Resilience can feel steady, even after hard days. Pain may still be present, but a sense of recovery feels possible.", "Staying calm in a crisis, talking yourself through hard moments, and not giving up on yourself.", "Inner resilience does not mean toughing everything out alone. It grows through rest, support and small wins.", ""],
  ["EMPOWERED BOUNDARY SETTING", "K", "A practice of stating your limits clearly and calmly, and keeping them.", "A clear boundary can feel nervous at first, then steadying. It can bring relief and a sense of self-respect.", "Saying what you will and will not do, calmly and without long apologies or excuses.", "A boundary is not a punishment. It states what you need, and others may respond with respect or with pushback.", ""],
  ["EMPOWERED CHOICE MAKING", "U", "Feeling free and able to choose what you will do, without needing permission.", "Choosing can feel clear and steady, with less pressure from others. Each choice you make builds confidence.", "Stating a preference, deciding on next steps, and owning the results of your choices.", "Choosing does not require being sure of every outcome. Confidence grows as you make choices and learn from them.", ""],
  ["EMPOWERED DECISION MAKING", "U", "Making decisions with clarity and ownership, and adjusting when new facts arrive.", "Decisions can feel clearer and less frightening. You may feel more in charge of where your life is heading.", "Gathering the key facts, asking for input, and setting a timeline for the choice.", "A good decision is not one that turns out perfectly. It is made with care, and it can be adjusted when the situation changes.", ""],
  ["EMPOWERED ENERGY PROTECTION", "K", "A practice of guarding your time and energy from people and tasks that drain you.", "Protecting energy can bring calm and a sense of control. Guilt may come first, then ease as the boundary holds.", "Choosing when to say yes, limiting draining contact, and planning rest into the week.", "Protecting your energy is not selfishness. It keeps you available for what matters most to you.", ""],
  ["EMPOWERED FUTURE SELF", "K", "A practice of picturing the person you want to become and making choices that support them.", "Imagining a future self can bring hope and motivation. It can also stir doubt when the goal feels far away.", "Writing a note to a future self, making small plans, and choosing daily habits that match goals.", "It is not a fantasy of becoming someone else. It links today's choices to the values you care about most.", ""],
  ["EMPOWERED MINDFUL PRESENCE", "K", "A practice of staying aware of the present moment with a calm sense of confidence.", "Presence can feel calm and steady. Worries still arise, but they lose some of their pull.", "Paying attention in conversations, noticing the breath, and responding instead of reacting.", "Mindfulness is not clearing the mind. It means noticing what is happening, then returning to the present again and again.", ""],
  ["EMPOWERED NO", "U", "The ability to say no clearly, protecting your time, energy and self-respect.", "Saying no can feel awkward at first, then freeing. It can bring relief from pressure you never agreed to carry.", "Declining a request calmly, without a long list of excuses or a rushed apology.", "A no does not need a long explanation. Kindness and firmness can go together.", ""],
  ["EMPOWERED PRESENCE", "U", "Showing up with calm confidence in a room, a meeting or a conversation.", "Presence can feel grounded and clear. It helps you speak and listen with less fear of being judged.", "A steady voice, attentive listening, and staying engaged in the moment without rushing.", "Presence is not dominating the room or being the loudest voice. It means being fully here and letting others be heard too.", ""],
  ["EMPOWERED RESILIENCE", "U", "Returning to your goals after a setback, learning from it and moving forward.", "After a setback, the first step can feel heavy. Each small step forward can rebuild confidence.", "Adjusting plans after a setback, asking for help, and continuing to show up.", "Resilience does not mean never struggling. It grows through support, rest and small steps taken one after another.", ""],
  ["EMPOWERED SELF-CARE", "K", "A practice of looking after your body, mind and needs as a daily skill.", "Self-care can feel grounding and worthwhile. At first it may feel selfish, then it starts to feel natural.", "Regular sleep, meals, breaks, and asking for help when it is needed.", "Self-care is not a reward for finishing everything first. It is basic upkeep that supports the rest of life.", ""],
  ["EMPOWERED SELF-EXPRESSION", "U", "Expressing your own voice, ideas and feelings with confidence and honesty.", "Speaking your truth can feel vulnerable and freeing at once. It can bring a sense of being fully yourself.", "Sharing opinions, making art or music, and speaking honestly in conversation.", "Self-expression does not mean sharing everything with everyone. You choose what to share, with whom and when.", ""],
  ["EMPOWERED VULNERABILITY", "U", "Being open about your needs and feelings from a place of inner strength.", "Opening up can feel tender and risky, and it can bring closeness. Strength can grow with each honest moment.", "Naming a feeling out loud, asking for help, and admitting a mistake without hiding it.", "Being open is strongest when it feels safe. You can choose who to trust and how much to share.", ""],
  ["ENABLER", "R", "A person who helps someone keep a harmful pattern going, often out of care or fear.", "The enabler may feel guilty, anxious or worn out. Helping can feel like the only way to keep the peace.", "Covering for someone, making excuses, or cleaning up consequences the person should face.", "Helping is not always enabling. The pattern is harmful when help keeps a problem going and blocks real change.", ""],
  ["ENCHANTED", "M", "A feeling of wonder and delight, as if the world has become a little magical.", "Enchantment can feel dreamy, warm and quietly thrilling. It can bring a sense of awe at ordinary things.", "Staring with wonder, speaking softly, or lingering over beautiful things.", "Enchantment does not need to be constant or grand. Small moments of wonder count and can be found in everyday life.", ""],
  ["ENERGIZED", "M", "Feeling lively, alert and ready to act, with energy to spare.", "Energy can feel bright and steady, with a wish to move, build or talk. Tasks may feel easier to start.", "Quick movements, eager talk, and starting projects with enthusiasm.", "Steady energy is healthy. Energy that feels unstoppable and comes with little sleep deserves a doctor's check.", ""],
  ["ENERGY SOVEREIGNTY", "U", "Feeling in charge of where your energy goes, and choosing how to spend it.", "Taking charge of your energy can feel steadier and less drained. Choices feel more like your own.", "Saying no to draining demands, and choosing activities that restore you.", "It does not mean ignoring responsibilities. It means planning energy with care, so that duties and rest both have a place.", ""],
  ["ENERGY VAMPIRE", "R", "An everyday phrase for someone whose complaints and conflicts leave others feeling drained.", "You may leave their company feeling tired or tense. Saying no can bring guilt, even when you need the space.", "Constant complaining, pulling others into conflict, or needing attention at every turn.", "Everyone can be draining on a hard day. The label is less useful than noticing a repeated pattern and choosing how much contact to keep.", ""],
  ["ENLIGHTENED", "U", "A sense of clear understanding and inner peace about what matters in life.", "Enlightenment can feel like clarity and calm, as if things make more sense. Life can feel more open and less rushed.", "Patience with others, openness to new ideas, and a steady, unhurried way of speaking.", "Feeling enlightened does not mean being above emotion or always right. Doubt, growth and mistakes still fit within it.", ""],
  ["ENMESHMENT", "R", "A close bond in which boundaries blur, and one person's feelings and choices merge with another's.", "The closeness can feel warm, but it can also feel smothering or hard to step away from. Your own choices may feel unclear.", "Constant checking in, speaking for each other, and strong distress when apart.", "Close family and friendships are healthy. Enmeshment is when closeness keeps each person from having a life and feelings of their own.", ""],
  ["ENTHRALLED", "F", "Completely captivated by something, with attention fully pulled in.", "Attention can feel fully held, and time may slip by unnoticed. It can bring joy and a sense of wonder.", "Staring, missing replies or appointments, and talking about the subject nonstop.", "Being enthralled is healthy when it brings joy. It becomes a problem when it crowds out sleep, work or people you care about.", ""],
  ["ENTHUSIASTIC", "M", "Eager and energetic about something you care about and want to share.", "Enthusiasm can feel warm, bright and full of energy. It can make tasks feel worth doing.", "Eager questions, quick starts, and sharing excitement with others.", "Enthusiasm does not need to last forever. Ups and downs are normal, and steady interest can grow from small wins.", ""],
  ["ENTITLED", "R", "Believing you deserve special treatment without needing to earn it.", "An entitled person may feel frustrated or angry when treated like everyone else. Others may feel used or overlooked.", "Expecting others to adjust to their needs, anger at small delays, and little thanks given.", "Wanting fair treatment is healthy. The pattern matters when the belief that rules do not apply to you harms others.", ""],
  ["ENTOMOMANIAC", "F", "A strong fascination with insects, such as collecting or studying them.", "Insects can feel fascinating, and studying them can bring deep satisfaction. Some people feel a strong urge to watch or collect them.", "Collecting specimens, sketching insects, or spending long hours watching them.", "Interest in insects is healthy and can lead to real science. It becomes a concern when it causes distress or harms animals.", ""],
  ["ENTOMOPHOBIA", "P", "A strong fear of insects, which can bring a sudden jolt of panic.", "Seeing an insect can bring a racing heart, sweating and a strong urge to run. The fear can feel out of proportion, even though it is very real.", "Avoiding gardens or parks, freezing near bugs, or running from them.", "It is a real fear, and it is more than disgust. Gradual exposure, guided by a therapist, can help many people feel calmer.", ""],
  ["ENVY", "M", "A painful feeling of wanting what someone else has, or wishing things were different.", "Envy can bring a sting and a tight chest. It can also show what you value.", "Comparing yourself to others, making small sharp comments, or pulling away from people who have more.", "Envy is a normal human feeling. It becomes harmful when it turns into resentment or a need to pull others down.", ""],
  ["EPHEBOPHILIA", "F", "A sexual interest in teenagers, who are still minors.", "This interest can bring shame, fear or confusion. Some people who have it feel ashamed or alone and do not know where to turn.", "Usually private. Others may notice a person seeking time alone with young people, which is a serious warning sign.", "Some people think an interest in teens is harmless or will simply pass. It is serious, and acting on it harms young people.", "Sexual interest in children is serious, and acting on it harms children. Stop It Now offers a free, confidential helpline for people worried about their own thoughts or behavior, or someone else's: 1-888-773-8368. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis, or call 911 if someone is in danger."],
  ["EPIDERMODYSPLASIA VERRUCIFORMIS", "B", "A rare inherited skin condition that causes wart-like patches and raises the risk of certain infections.", "Skin patches can feel itchy or sore, and treatment can take a lot of time. The condition can bring worry and frustration.", "Regular skin checks, visits to specialists, and careful protection from the sun.", "It is not caused by poor hygiene or anything a person did wrong. Dermatology care, infection checks and sun protection can help manage it.", ""],
  ["ERGOMANIAC", "V", "A compulsive urge to work, often at the cost of health, sleep and relationships.", "Work can feel like the only safe place, and rest may bring guilt or restlessness. Stopping can feel almost impossible.", "Working late and on weekends, cutting into sleep, and avoiding time off.", "Hard work is not a flaw. The pattern becomes harmful when work takes over health, relationships and rest, and it often needs support to change.", ""],
  ["EROTOMANIAC", "D", "A fixed false belief that a stranger or a person of higher status secretly loves you.", "The belief can feel completely true, and every small sign may seem to confirm it. Attempts to correct it may bring anger or panic.", "Repeated messages or contact, following the person, and insisting the feeling is mutual.", "The person is not simply lying or being romantic. It is a sign of an illness that responds to treatment, and care can bring relief.", "You can call or text 988 in the US now for support. You can also call 911 or go to an emergency room if someone is in danger."],
  ["EROTOPHILE", "F", "A person with an open, positive attitude toward sex and erotic material.", "Openness about sex can feel natural and freeing. For some, it may bring mixed feelings, especially if they were raised to feel shame.", "Relaxed talk about sex, interest in erotic books or films, and comfort with sexual topics.", "Being open about sex is not the same as having a problem. Healthy sexuality rests on consent, respect and honesty.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."],
  ["ETERNAL SELF-COMMITMENT", "U", "A lasting commitment to your own growth and to the values you hold most dearly.", "Commitment can feel steady and meaningful. It can carry you through rough patches when motivation fades.", "Keeping promises to yourself, returning to goals, and acting on your values over time.", "Commitment does not mean never slipping. It means finding your way back to your path after setbacks.", ""],
  ["ETERNAL SELF-LOVE", "U", "A steady, lasting love for yourself that holds through every season of life.", "Self-love can feel warm, steady and protective. Over time it can soften harsh inner voices.", "Kind self-talk, honoring your limits, and caring for your needs without guilt.", "Loving yourself does not mean ignoring problems. It means facing them without cruelty toward yourself.", ""],
  ["ETERNAL WHOLENESS", "U", "A deep sense of being complete, even while life keeps changing around you.", "Wholeness can bring peace and a feeling of being enough. Pieces of life can feel connected rather than split apart.", "Speaking honestly, accepting mixed feelings, and living in line with what matters most.", "Feeling whole does not mean having no struggles. It means no part of you has to be hidden.", ""],
  ["EUPHORIC", "M", "A surge of intense, almost overwhelming happiness that can feel unreal.", "Euphoria can feel light, electric and almost dreamlike. It often fades quickly, sometimes leaving a flat or tired feeling.", "Laughing freely, talking fast, or making big plans all at once.", "A brief euphoric moment is part of normal life. Repeated intense highs with little sleep may signal a mood disorder that needs a doctor.", ""],
  ["EVISCEROPHILE", "F", "A fascination with gore and the insides of the body, linked to sexual arousal for some people.", "Images of injury can feel gripping, and some people feel a strong pull toward them. Shame or confusion often comes with it.", "Seeking graphic content, lingering on injuries, or keeping the interest hidden from others.", "Having an interest is not the same as hurting anyone. Acting on it in ways that harm others is serious, and help is available.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis. If someone is in danger, call 911."],
  ["EXAGGERATED STARTLE RESPONSE", "B", "A jump or flinch that is stronger and longer than usual at sudden sounds or movements.", "A sudden sound can cause a big jolt, a racing heart and tension that lasts a while. It can feel embarrassing afterward.", "Jumping at small noises, flinching at quick movements, or freezing when surprised.", "It is a normal alarm system working too hard, often after stress or trauma. Relaxation skills and therapy can calm it over time.", ""],
  ["EXCITED", "M", "Eager, alert and looking forward to something that is coming up.", "Excitement can feel bright, fast and full of energy. The body may buzz with anticipation before an event.", "Talking fast, smiling often, and planning next steps eagerly.", "Excitement is healthy, and it naturally rises and falls with events. Feeling wired with little sleep for many days is worth raising with a doctor.", ""],
  ["EXCORIATION DISORDER", "D", "A condition of repeated skin picking that leaves sores, scabs or scars.", "Picking can bring relief or focus, then shame or regret. The urge can feel hard to resist, even when it hurts.", "Sores, scabs or healing marks, and long stretches of time spent picking at the skin.", "It is treatable, and it is not a sign of weakness. Specialized therapy can help many people reduce picking.", "If picking feels like self-harm, or you have thoughts of ending your life, call or text 988 in the US now, and call 911 if you are in danger."],
  ["EXECUTIVE DYSFUNCTION", "D", "Trouble planning, starting, organizing and finishing tasks, often linked to stress, injury, illness or attention conditions.", "Starting even simple tasks can feel stuck. Frustration and shame can pile up when plans fall apart.", "Missed deadlines, unfinished projects, and trouble keeping track of time and papers.", "It is not laziness or a lack of intelligence. It has real causes, and strategies, support and a medical check can help.", ""],
  ["EXHIBITIONISM", "F", "A sexual interest in being seen by strangers who have not agreed to it.", "The urge can feel strong and hard to ignore, with shame or fear afterward. The person may keep it hidden for a long time.", "Reports from strangers who were surprised, and sometimes police contact.", "Having an urge is not the same as acting on it. Acting on it harms people who did not agree to be seen, and it is illegal in most places.", "A therapist who works with sexual health can help you talk it through. In the US, call or text 988 if you are in crisis."]
];
